{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg1Lsrif2BFR1kGVBj9bo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I give up. The formal statement is false for every n ≥ 5, so no proof exists, and I left the file unchanged.\n\nThe problem is in the formalization:\n\n1. `touches_only_vertices s1 s2` says every point in `s1.occupy ∩ s2.occupy` is a vertex of both squares. For disjoint squares this holds vacuously.\n2. The first conjunct requires this for every pair `n1 ≠ n2`. Under that conjunct, every other square `i ≠ m` satisfies `touches_only_vertices m i`.\n3. The square `m` itself never satisfies it. Its occupied region is a nondegenerate square, which is not contained in its four vertices.\n4. The squares have side lengths `1, …, n`, so they are pairwise distinct as `Square` values. The set in the second conjunct therefore has exactly `n − 1` elements.\n5. The second conjunct requires that set to have `ncard = 2`, so `n − 1 = 2`, i.e. `n = 3`. This contradicts `n ≥ 5`.\n\nSo for every `n ≥ 5` and every `position`, the conjunction is false, and the existential cannot be proved. The intended problem requires that squares touch only at vertices, meaning each square touches exactly two others. The formalization instead counts every disjoint square as \"touching\", which makes the second conjunct impossible."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":834,"cache_read_input_tokens":1887,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":834,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ee2896e1-97ab-4813-9308-baa4c503a8f3","uuid":"b0f81473-e795-42c4-97ca-ca2d5ae37ced","timestamp":"2026-10-14T02:30:41.009Z","request_id":"req_011Cg1LsrPowVyYoRnkhAobJ"}
